            <div xml:id="d178">

                <pb facs="0349" n="259" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg259"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5131/922: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France (Edge)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-12-10T11:00:00-05:00">December 10, 1932—11
                        a.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>432. Your 705, Dec. 10, 10 a.m. As soon as the debt situation has passed its
                    present acute phase, I wish that you would telegraph me whether you feel that
                    continuing negotiations for a commercial treaty can be kept separate in the
                    French mind from “other forms of tangible compensation available for the
                    expansion of markets for products of American agriculture and labor,” to which
                    reference is made in our note to the French.</p>
                <p>My remaining preoccupation just now is not to prejudice in any way the ultimate
                    settlement of the debt problem. If you see a way to maintain the independent
                    character of these commercial negotiations, I am prepared to recommend to the
                    President to send you full powers and leave to your discretion the decision of
                    accepting such of the French proposals as would result in a treaty which would
                    sufficiently improve the existing situation from the point of view of American
                    trade as a whole to justify the departures which it would involve from our
                    general treaty policy.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
